[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Dependency Service and Platform-Specific Implementation in Xamarin"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cross-platform development frameworks like Xamarin.Forms allow developers to write shared UI and business logic. However, there are times when you need to access native platform APIs, such as device sensors, local databases, or hardware features. Xamarin solves this architectural challenge using the DependencyService mechanism."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding DependencyService"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"DependencyService enables shared code to invoke platform-specific implementations. It acts as a service locator, resolving interfaces defined in the portable or shared project to their concrete implementations residing in the platform-specific projects (Android, iOS, etc.)."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Define the Interface in Shared Code"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The first step is to create an interface in your shared Xamarin.Forms project. This interface outlines the contract for the native functionality you want to expose."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public interface ITextToSpeech
{
    void Speak(string text);
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Implement the Interface on Android"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, implement the interface in your Android project. You must decorate the class with the [assembly: Dependency(typeof(...))] attribute so that Xamarin can register and discover the implementation at runtime."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"[assembly: Dependency(typeof(TextToSpeechAndroid))]
namespace MyProject.Droid
{
    public class TextToSpeechAndroid : ITextToSpeech
    {
        public void Speak(string text)
        {
            var context = Android.App.Application.Context;
            // Android specific text-to-speech implementation
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Implement the Interface on iOS"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Similarly, provide the iOS-specific implementation in the iOS project using the same assembly attribute."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"[assembly: Dependency(typeof(TextToSpeechIOS))]
namespace MyProject.iOS
{
    public class TextToSpeechIOS : ITextToSpeech
    {
        public void Speak(string text)
        {
            // iOS specific AVSpeechSynthesizer implementation
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Consume the Service in Shared Code"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"With the platform implementations in place, you can now call the native functionality from your shared Xamarin.Forms business logic or ViewModel using DependencyService.Get<T>()."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public void OnButtonClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    DependencyService.Get<ITextToSpeech>().Speak("Hello from Xamarin Dependency Service");
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always ensure your platform-specific classes have a parameterless constructor, as DependencyService instantiates them dynamically."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep interface definitions decoupled from any native UI libraries to maintain clean architectural boundaries."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider migrating to Dependency Injection (DI) containers like Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection for more complex applications."}]
